ndered video) and think “look how smooth it runs!” — then notice the actual gameplay is less detailed or struggles to maintain FPS. That’s simply the divide between offline and real-time rendering. The same GPU that “plays” a movie effortlessly is only displaying frames that were already created with enormous computational effort. When it has to *create* those frames live, the workload is orders of magnitude higher. In short: **Movies are playback; games are live, interactive construction.** That’s why graphics cards sweat during gameplay but breeze through movies.
