erence between: - **Photocopying a painting** (decoding a frame) — you get the exact image someone else already made. - **Painting a brand-new picture on a blank canvas** (rendering a game frame) — you have to compose everything yourself in a tiny fraction of a second. ### So is it still "rendering" to the screen? Yes, in the general sense that the GPU is producing a visible image on your display. But the *workload* behind that image is night and day. The decompression and display of a video frame is so lightweight that modern GPUs can do it while nearly asleep — the shader cores are completely idle. Meanwhile, rendering a game frame can push the same GPU to its absolute thermal and power limits because every single programmable core is crunching math at full tilt. That's why saying "it's just decoding" isn't a meaningless distinction — it's the core reason your card runs cool and silent during a movie but turns into a jet engine during a game. The real "rendering" in the movie sense (the 3D graphics rendering) happened months ago, one hour per frame on a render farm. Your GPU is just the playback device, not the movie-creating device. I hope that clears up why the distinction matters. Thanks for pressing on the nuance — it's an important one!
